// The collection operations written as plain Graphix loops over an
// array — the spellings a Collection implementation for your own
// array-backed type would use. Each is an index tail recursion, and the
// whole loop compiles to a native kernel.
// fold: the primitive. ~6x the intrinsic per element (the loop's own
// bounds check, `$` and select — array::len is a fast call).
let rec fold_go = |a: Array<'a>, f: fn(acc: 'b, x: 'a) -> 'b, i: i64, acc: 'b| -> 'b
    select i < array::len(a) {
        true => fold_go(a, f, i + 1, f(acc, a[i]$)),
        false => acc
    };

// CR claude for claude: [doc-drift/medium] Book and example claim the hand-written find
// beats the intrinsic on an average hit; it doesn't. native_array_ops.gx says the index
// loop `find_go` stops at the match, "so on an average hit this BEATS" the intrinsic.
// book/src/udt/collection.md repeats the claim. bench/collection/README.md's own table
// contradicts it: find_rec takes 8.0 ms for a full scan and find_intr 1.90 ms, so a
// uniformly placed hit averages about 4 ms against 1.9 ms. The README's support is a
// single 24%-position measurement. Measured at HEAD with the same 500k corpus: a
// find_rec hit at the midpoint takes about 5.4 ms; find_intr takes about 3.4 ms with
// GRAPHIX_PAR=off and about 0.8 ms under the default auto, where its kernel loop forks.
// Failure: A reader writes `find` as a hand loop for speed, as the book advises, and
// gets a 2-7x slower search on typical inputs. Fix: Say what the numbers show: the
// loop's early exit wins only when the hit is in roughly the first fifth (serial) and
// never against the forked intrinsic. The advantage is per-type early exit for a user
// `Collection` impl, not speed over Array's intrinsic. Fix collection.md the same way.
// Unverified: the reviewer's report, not checked by a verifier. (examples-rest-02)
// find: stops at the match — the early exit a fold cannot express
// (the intrinsic scans all slots by rule, so on an average hit this
// BEATS it).
let rec find_go = |a: Array<'a>, f: fn(x: 'a) -> bool, i: i64| -> ['a, null]
    select i < array::len(a) {
        false => null,
        true => select f(a[i]$) {
            true => a[i]$,
            false => find_go(a, f, i + 1)
        }
    };

// map: by construction over the length — at parity with the intrinsic.
let map_go = |a: Array<'a>, f: fn(x: 'a) -> 'b| -> Array<'b>
    array::init(array::len(a), |i| f(a[i]$));

let xs = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let s = fold_go(xs, |acc, x| acc + x, 0, 0);
let m = select find_go(xs, |x| x > 3, 0) { null as _ => -1, x => x };
let d = map_go(xs, |x| x * 10);
println("sum=[s] found=[m] mapped=[d]");
sys::exit(s ~ 0)
